<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="linoleate biosynthesis I (plants)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5995" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: LINOLEIC_ACID| is a polyunsaturated fatty acid that is abundant in many vegetable oils, especially safflower and sunflower oils. It is an essential dietary requirement for all mammals, that lack the &amp;Delta;&lt;sup&gt;12&lt;/sup&gt; desaturase that produces it. A few animals are able to synthesize |FRAME: LINOLEIC_ACID|, as described in |FRAME: PWY-6001|. Linoleate is an &amp;omega;-6 fatty acid, referring to the position of the first double bond from the methyl end of the fatty acid. In all plant tissues, the major glycerolipids are first synthesized using only palmitoyl (16:0) and oleoyl (18:1) acyl groups, the products of plastidic saturated fatty acid synthesis (see |FRAME: PWY-5971|, |FRAME: PWY-5989| and |FRAME: PWY-5147|). Subsequent desaturation of the fatty acids to the highly unsaturated forms typical of the membranes of plant cells is carried while they are already attached to lipids. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; Most of the oleoyl-[acp] is released from the [acp]-moiety and exported from the plastid to the endoplasmic reticulum (ER), and is converted to |FRAME: OLEOYL-COA| in the process by the plastidic |FRAME: AT1G77590-MONOMER|. The |FRAME: OLEOYL-COA| is incorporated into lipids, forming |FRAME: L-PHOSPHATIDATE phosphatidylglycerols|, |FRAME: Diacylglycerides diglycerides|, and |FRAME: PHOSPHATIDYLCHOLINE phosphatidylcholines|. Further desaturation of the oleoyl groups to linoleoyl groups in the ER occurrs while incorporated into lipids, and is catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-1.14.19.22||, encoded by the |FRAME: AT3G12120| gene. A smaller portion of the oleoyl-[acp] molecules can be incorporated into |FRAME: L-PHOSPHATIDATE phosphatidylglycerols| and |FRAME: Diacylglycerides diglycerides| within the chloroplast. In this case, the desaturation to |FRAME: Linoleoyl-groups| occurs by a plastidic desaturase (|FRAME: EC-1.14.19.23|), which is encoded by the |FRAME: G-10795| gene. Envelope membranes isolated from chloroplasts of spinach (|FRAME: TAX-3562|) could desaturate oleoyl groups in monogalactosyl diacylglycerol to linoleoyl groups. The desaturation required NADPH in combination with ferredoxin, and was also observed with other biosynthetic intermediates such as phosphatidates |CITS: [11607123]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="SRI" />
    <Author name="caspi" />
  </Authors>
